rust-proxmox-apt-history (0.1.2-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * history: tell whether a transaction without end time is still running or
    got interrupted. As APT runs one transaction at a time, only the newest
    one can still be running, which it is if the process holding the lock of
    the dpkg frontend started before it. If that cannot be told, like when
    the same command runs again or without permission to check the lock, the
    state stays open as before.

 -- Proxmox Support Team <support@proxmox.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 03:46:43 +0100

rust-proxmox-apt-history (0.1.1-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * update changelogs: show that no changelog is available for packages of
    repositories without a changelog location, like most third-party ones.
    APT's message for these was not recognized, so a single such package made
    each request resolve every package on its own, which could use up the
    time limit with many pending updates. Such packages also showed as missing
    from the package lists, and the daily prefetch counted them as failed.

  * update changelogs: keep the cached changelogs if resolving their locations
    failed, for example at the time limit, instead of emptying the cache. An
    APT failure that is not about a package, like a broken list of sources,
    now shows APT's error instead of asking to refresh the updates.

  * update changelogs: write the time of the last request and the markers of
    slow downloads atomically, so that a daily prefetch running at the same
    time cannot take the cache as unused and empty it.

  * api: answer failures to read the logs with a server error instead of a
    client error.

  * api: refuse more than four requests that read the logs at the same time
    with a 503 error, as each can hold up to 128 MiB, instead of letting them
    pile up.

  * update changelogs: report the same failure in the summary of the daily
    prefetch each time, if several packages failed.

  * terminal output: ignore the parameters of underline colors instead of
    taking them as styles of their own, which could, for example, make text
    bold.

  * update changelogs: set the permissions of the download directory
    regardless of the umask. A restrictive one, like in a hardened shell, made
    APT download to /tmp instead, where the size limit of a download does not
    apply and stopped downloads leave their directories behind.

 -- Proxmox Support Team <support@proxmox.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 01:37:59 +0100

rust-proxmox-apt-history (0.1.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * initial release of proxmox-apt-history, a crate for parsing the history
    and terminal logs that APT keeps, so the products can show what changed
    on a system and when, for example to correlate a regression with an
    update.

  * list the transactions of all rotated logs newest first, with their package
    changes, command line, requesting user and error. Their terminal output
    can be read as plain text or keeping basic text styles. Damaged logs are
    read with bounded memory.

  * mark the transactions that changed the major release of Debian or Ceph,
    and provide the changelog version range of each package change.

  * read the changelogs of all package changes of a transaction at once,
    grouped by source package, with an API method for it, so a web UI can
    show them together with a single request.

  * download the changelogs of pending updates from the package repositories,
    grouped and limited to the new versions like for a transaction, to show
    what an upgrade brings before installing it. A cache keeps them, so that a
    request continues where the previous one reached its time limit, and the
    products can fill it after refreshing the package lists.

  * the optional `api-types` feature provides the API types, also on wasm32
    for the web UIs, `impl` the library functions and `api` the API methods
    that the Rust based products can mount.

 -- Proxmox Support Team <support@proxmox.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 02:32:15 +0100
